<h3 id="rls">The Retirement Living Standards</h3>

<p>The Retirement Living Standards are produced by the same research centre for Pensions UK. They describe three levels: minimum, moderate and comfortable.</p>

{% call m.scrollTable() %}
<table>
  <caption>Retirement Living Standards: yearly spending, UK outside London, April 2025 prices</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Standard</th><th scope="col" class="num">One person</th><th scope="col" class="num">Two people</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Minimum</th><td class="num">{{ m.f("rls-min-single") }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.f("rls-min-couple") }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Moderate</th><td class="num">{{ m.f("rls-mod-single") }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.f("rls-mod-couple") }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Comfortable</th><td class="num">{{ m.f("rls-comf-single") }}</td><td class="num">{{ m.f("rls-comf-couple") }}</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endcall %}
{{ m.sourceNote("rls-mod-single" | figure) }}

<p>These figures leave out rent and mortgage payments. {{ m.c("rls-housing-assumption") }} They are spending, so the income needed before tax may be higher if tax is due. {{ m.c("rls-after-tax") }}</p>

<p>The minimum standard and the Minimum Income Standard are not two separate pieces of evidence. The minimum standard is the Minimum Income Standard pensioner budget for 2025. If they agree, that is because they are the same research.</p>

<h2 id="observed">What retired households spend</h2>

{{ m.dataTable("observed-spending") }}

<p>Read this table with four points in mind.</p>
<ul>
  <li>These are averages over every household in the group, including those that spent nothing on an item.</li>
  <li>The survey's samples are small. {{ m.c("fs-small-samples") }} The groups here are smaller still. The group of two adults mainly dependent on state pensions had {{ tables["observed-spending"].rows[0][2] }} households in the sample, so its figures are weak.</li>
  <li>Households that depend mainly on state pensions spend less than other retired households. The table cannot show whether that is because they need less or because they have less.</li>
  <li>The categories are not the same as those in the research budgets, so the two cannot be compared line by line. Council Tax, for example, is inside "Other expenditure items" here.</li>
</ul>

