<h2 id="own-needs">What one household needs</h2>

<dl>
  <dt>Housing</dt>
  <dd>The largest difference between households. An owner with no mortgage pays no rent but pays for repairs and buildings insurance. A renter's costs depend on the area and on whether the landlord is a council, a housing association or private. Taking the rent out of a renter's budget does not make an owner's budget.</dd>
  <dt>Sharing costs</dt>
  <dd>Two people do not need twice what one person needs. Heating, Council Tax and rent are shared. When a partner dies, income often falls by more than costs do.</dd>
  <dt>Where the household lives</dt>
  <dd>The research budgets are for urban areas outside London. London costs more. Rural households often need a car where the minimum budgets assume buses.</dd>
  <dt>Energy</dt>
  <dd>A person who is at home all day, or who is ill, needs more heating. An older or poorly insulated home costs more to heat.</dd>
  <dt>Disability and care</dt>
  <dd>No research budget on this page includes them. They can be large. Attendance Allowance exists to help with them and has to be claimed. <a href="/state-pension/#extra-help">Help with the costs of disability</a>.</dd>
  <dt>Costs that come now and then</dt>
  <dd>A cooker, a boiler repair, new glasses. The research budgets include a weekly share for replacing things. That spreads the cost on paper. It does not mean the household has the cash on the day the cooker breaks.</dd>
</dl>

<h2 id="prices">Prices and pensioner households</h2>

<p>The inflation rate is how fast prices are rising now. It is not how much prices have risen in total. When inflation falls from 10% to 3%, prices are still rising, on top of the earlier rise.</p>

<p>The Office for National Statistics publishes a measure of costs for different types of household.</p>

